        "source":  "MIT Technology Review",
        "url":  "https://www.technologyreview.com/2026/07/30/1140927/a-fundamental-flaw-leaves-llms-vulnerable-to-attack/",
        "date":  "2026-07-30",
        "theme":  "Models",
        "summary":  "Researchers at the International Conference on Machine Learning revealed that LLMs identify instruction sources by text style and vocabulary rather than by role tags, creating an architectural vulnerability that cannot be fully patched through training alone. Attackers can craft text mimicking chain-of-thought reasoning to manipulate models from OpenAI, Anthropic, Alibaba, and DeepSeek into executing harmful requests. The flaw has been demonstrated producing instructions for synthesizing dangerous substances and sabotaging navigation systems.",
        "execSummary":  "Every major LLM platform — including OpenAI, Anthropic, Alibaba, and DeepSeek — shares a fundamental architectural flaw that makes models persistently vulnerable to prompt injection attacks. The vulnerability stems from how models parse instruction sources and cannot be eliminated through training or red-teaming alone. For enterprise leaders deploying AI in customer-facing or sensitive operational roles, this finding reinforces the need for layered guardrails, human-in-the-loop checkpoints, and strict input sanitization beyond what any single vendor provides. Sole reliance on model-level safety is insufficient.",
